  "track": "track-03",
  "title": "injection safety permissions roles and authentication",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Authentication proves a connection identity; authorization maps principals and roles to allowed actions on securable objects. A successful login does not imply broad database access.",
    "SQL Server permissions can be granted, denied, or revoked at different scopes. Least privilege and role-based assignment reduce the impact of compromised application credentials.",
    "Parameterized queries separate data from SQL syntax. Dynamic identifiers cannot be value parameters and require allowlists and safe identifier quoting; escaping-only approaches are fragile.",
    "Stored procedures do not automatically prevent injection if they concatenate untrusted input. Execute context, ownership chaining, cross-database trust, and service identities affect the effective permission boundary.",
    "Encryption in transit, auditing, secret management, and server-side authorization are separate controls; none substitutes for secure query construction."
  ],
